The correct answer is (d). Statement (d) reflects radical behaviorism (associated with Watson and Skinner), which Bandura explicitly critiqued and moved beyond. Social Learning Theory (later Social Cognitive Theory) was developed precisely to address the limitations of radical behaviorism by establishing that:
1. People learn through observational modeling (Statement a);
2. Learning is an internal cognitive process that may remain latent without producing an immediate overt behavioral change (Statement b);
3. Cognitive processes, expectations, and self-efficacy beliefs mediate human functioning (Statement c).
Claiming that all behavior is exclusively determined by direct conditioning ignores cognitive mediation and observational learning.
